[agent] ## Summary
[agent] Registered feature group **`scaled56ae89`** version 1 with:
[agent] - **Record key:** `row_id`
[agent] - **Columns:** `row_id, split, f1, f2, f3, f4`
[agent] - **Online enabled:** yes (low-latency lookup available, offline + online stores)
[agent] - **650 rows:** 400 `train` + 250 `serve`
[agent] **How the work ran (all platform-side):**
[agent] 1. Staged the raw `features_train.csv` (400 rows) and `features_serve.csv` (250 rows) into two helper feature groups via `hops fg insert`.
[agent] 2. Computed the standardization entirely in **Trino SQL** (`hops sql`): train-only `avg()` and `stddev_pop()` (population std, no Bessel correction) per feature, applied `(x - mean)/std` to **both** splits using the train statistics, tagged each row's `split`, and `round(…, 6)`.
[agent] 3. Wrote the result into the online-enabled FG via `hops fg insert`.
[agent] **Verification** (read back through the platform): train split has per-feature mean ≈ 0 and population-std = 1.0 (confirming train-only standardization), while serve rows are shifted (avg_f1 ≈ 2.46) as expected since they use train statistics. Counts: 400 train / 250 serve.
[agent] Note on the interface: the offline FG tables are Delta Lake, so the Trino queries required `--catalog delta` and `--no-verify` (the cluster serves an IP-SAN-mismatched cert). The standardization computation never ran locally — it executed in the platform's Trino engine; the JSON only transited the local file as `fg insert`'s normal input.
[agent] Two staging feature groups (`raw_train_56ae89`, `raw_serve_56ae89`) remain in the store as scratch; they're harmless and not part of the deliverable.
